# Production readiness (strict)

Ship code and designs that are safe under **real traffic, failures, and multi-tenant load**—not only the happy path in local dev.

Cross-read when relevant: [server-express-conventions.mdc](./server-express-conventions.mdc), [application-stack.mdc](./application-stack.mdc), [docs/features/operational-hardening.md](./docs/features/operational-hardening.md).

---

## 1. Tenancy & security (non-negotiable)

- **Org scope from URL** for workspace APIs: `/api/org/:orgId/*` + `requireOrgAccess`. Never trust `organizationId` from body/query alone on authenticated routes.
- **Service role** stays server-only; client uses anon key + RLS. No secrets in client bundles or logs.
- **AuthZ before work**: check membership/role (`ADMIN`) before mutations; fail closed (403/401), not silent no-ops.
- **Public ingress** (webhooks, `messages/incoming`): validate payloads, idempotency keys, rate limits; never log raw PII or tokens.
- **Input bounds**: max body size (already 1mb), string lengths, UUID format, enum/status checks—reject early with 400.
- **Production errors**: generic 5xx messages to clients; detail in server logs only (`NODE_ENV=production`).

---

## 2. Reliability & failure modes

Design every async path for: **timeout, partial failure, duplicate delivery, process crash**.

| Pattern | Requirement |
|---------|-------------|
| HTTP handlers | Do not block on email/AI/heavy work; enqueue (`automation_jobs`) or fire-and-forget analytics with safe catch |
| Idempotency | Webhooks and ingress: dedupe keys; DB unique constraints where duplicates are costly |
| Retries | Exponential backoff + `max_attempts` + dead state; never infinite retry loops |
| DB writes | Handle `23514` / constraint errors as 400; distinguish 404 vs 500 |
| External APIs | Timeouts, treat provider errors as retryable vs fatal; record failure reason |
| Graceful shutdown | HTTP: `server.close()` on SIGTERM/SIGINT; workers: finish current job, stop accepting new work |

**Edge cases to explicitly handle:**

- Duplicate webhook / double-click send
- Assignee deleted or wrong org
- Org settings missing → sane defaults, log once
- Migration/table missing → degrade without crashing the whole request (only where already established, e.g. analytics)
- Concurrent updates (last-write-wins vs optimistic locking—document choice)

---

## 3. Scalability & bottlenecks

Before merging, ask: **what grows unbounded?**

| Risk | Mitigation |
|------|------------|
| N+1 queries | Batch/select; avoid per-row round trips in loops |
| Large lists | Pagination (`limit`/`cursor`), indexes on filter/sort columns |
| Hot rows | Avoid serializing all org traffic on one row; use queues |
| Rate limits | **Redis only** (`REDIS_URL` required); shared across API replicas—do not reintroduce in-memory limit Maps |
| Full table scans | Reports/analytics: date bounds, rollups, indexes on `(organization_id, created_at)` |
| Realtime fan-out | Scope channels by org/conversation; don’t broadcast global |
| Worker poll interval | Tune `AUTOMATION_POLL_MS` / batch size; SLA jobs must not starve notify jobs |
| LLM (future) | Per-org + per-user rate limits, token budgets, timeouts, circuit breaker |

**Multi-process layout (this repo):**

- API (`server.js`) and **automation worker** are separate processes—`npm run start` does **not** start the worker; document/deploy both.
- Cron (SLA scan) needs `AUTOMATION_CRON_SECRET` and external scheduler.

---

## 4. Observability

- **Structured logs** for failures (JSON one-liners): `organization_id`, `conversation_id`, `error_code`—see `outboundDeliveryMonitor.service.js`.
- **Product events**: emit `support_events` for lifecycle, outbound sent/failed, SLA breach—enables Reports and alerting.
- **No silent swallow** except intentional fire-and-forget; always `console.warn`/`error` with context when skipping.
- **Health**: keep `GET /health` cheap; deeper checks (DB ping) optional separate route.
- **Metrics/alerts (ops)**: document how to detect spikes in `message.outbound_failed`, queue depth, job `dead` status.

---

## 5. Data integrity

- Migrations: reversible where possible; `IF NOT EXISTS`; comments on JSONB/settings columns.
- **JSONB settings**: merge with shared defaults (`mergeOrgAiSettings`); never replace entire document blindly.
- **Feature flags**: respect `organizations.settings` and `conversations.ai_enabled` before AI/automation side effects.
- **Transactions**: when multiple tables must stay consistent, use RPC or explicit transaction pattern—don’t leave orphan rows.
- **Cascade deletes**: rely on FK `on delete cascade` consciously; soft-delete only if product requires it.

---

## 6. API & client contract

- Stable error shape: `{ error: string }` + correct HTTP status.
- **429** for rate limits with `Retry-After` when known.
- Version org-scoped routes; deprecate global stubs (e.g. prefer `/api/org/:orgId/ai/assist`).
- Client: handle loading/error/empty states; don’t assume realtime always connected.

---

## 7. Production checklist (apply before marking work done)

- [ ] Happy path + main failure paths tested (or reason noted)
- [ ] Org isolation verified on new endpoints
- [ ] No unbounded memory/CPU (loops, Maps, unbounded `select *`)
- [ ] Background work queued or async with idempotency
- [ ] Env vars documented in `server/.env.example` (no secrets committed)
- [ ] User-facing behavior reflected in `docs/IMPLEMENTED-FEATURES.md` + relevant `docs/features/*.md` when behavior changes
- [ ] Rate limits on public and costly endpoints (AI, ingress, webhooks)

---

## 8. Anti-patterns (reject in review)

- Global `/api/*` mutations without org scope
- Sync email/LLM in request thread for high-latency providers
- Storing API keys in `organizations.settings` without encryption story
- In-memory rate limit Maps (use Redis via `rateLimitRedis.js`)
- “Best effort” deletes/updates with no error surfacing
- Loading entire conversation history for AI without token/limit cap (future phases)

---

## 9. When to escalate design

Pause implementation and propose a short design note when adding:

- New queue/worker type, cron, or cross-service dependency
- New PII store, export, or retention policy
- Billing/metering, multi-region, or >1 API replica defaults
- Autonomous customer-visible AI sends (Phase 6)—needs gates, audit, kill switch

Prefer extending existing services (`conversationUpdate`, `enqueueAutomationJob`, `emitSupportEvent`) over parallel ad-hoc paths.
